window.docContents[18].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry--Global-Sobolev-regularity":{"contents":"For homogeneous Dirichlet data and , the principal global theorem says\n\n\n\nunder the operational  atlas, global  coefficient\nrepresentatives, and pointwise uniform ellipticity. The assembled\nrepresentative agrees almost everywhere with the original weak solution.\n\nBounded first- and zeroth-order coefficients are then moved into an effective\n forcing, giving full-operator  regularity. At arbitrary order,\nthe proof combines principal regularity with the lower-order reduction and\nderives the preceding Sobolev regularity by induction. The chart, coefficient,\nand forcing hypotheses remain explicit.\n\nFor every , the arbitrary-order principal theorem proves\n\n\n\nwith forward charts of class , inverse charts of class\n, coherent global  coefficient representatives, and\nuniform ellipticity. Under the same principal and atlas hypotheses,\nfirst- and zeroth-order coefficients in  give the full\nshifted-operator result. It starts with the homogeneous-Dirichlet weak\nsolution in  and constructs a  representative.\nPrevious-order regularity and the principal regularity implication are\nproved within the argument.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Regularity and geometry","header":"5.7.1.3.3. Global Sobolev regularity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry--Global-Sobolev-regularity"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Why-distributions-matter":{"contents":"Solutions of hyperbolic PDEs can develop shocks (discontinuities in finite time), elliptic\nregularity theory must handle forcing terms in  rather than ,\nand fundamental solutions like the Newtonian potential are singular at the\norigin. The theory of distributions, created by Laurent Schwartz (1950,\nThéorie des distributions), extends differentiation to all these objects by\nreplacing the pointwise derivative with a duality pairing against smooth test\nfunctions.\n\nThe starting point is integration by parts. If  is smooth and  is\ncompactly supported, then  with no boundary\nterms. The right-hand side makes sense even when  is merely locally\nintegrable, or even when it is not a function at all but only a linear\nfunctional on test functions. By declaring the right-hand side to be the\ndefinition of the distributional derivative, one obtains a differentiation\ntheory that:\n\n* applies to every locally integrable function, including those with jumps;* produces the Dirac mass as the derivative of the Heaviside step function;* supplies the language in which singular fundamental solutions are stated;* supports Fourier analysis after passing to tempered distributions.\n\nA test function is a smooth, localized probe. The Dirac mass records its\nvalue at one point: . Its derivative records\nthe negative slope there:\n. Neither operation asks for a pointwise\nvalue of the distribution itself. Equality of distributions means agreement\non every such probe.\n\nApproximation is the other route to the same objects. Convolve a rough\nfunction with a smooth kernel of shrinking width and you get smooth\nfunctions converging to it, which is how the Sobolev chapter carries\nclassical calculus into the weak setting.\n\n\n\nAt the jump, each smoothed profile is continuous while the limiting step is\nnot. We therefore need a mode of convergence that tolerates this discrepancy:\nlocal integral convergence, or convergence after pairing with a test function.\nThis is why the weak-derivative argument later estimates the approximation\nin a norm instead of requiring uniform agreement at every point.\n\n treats mollification in Appendix C.5;\nthe theory of distributions is due to Schwartz (1950, 1951). We follow Mathlib's\nformalization, which constructs test functions as smooth compactly supported\nfunctions and distributions as their continuous dual.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Canonical distributions","header":"3.4.1.1. Why distributions matter","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Why-distributions-matter"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Keller___Segel-Virial-Horizon--Two-dimensional-Keller___Segel-virial-horizon":{"contents":"The virial mechanism turns a question about how long a PDE solution lives into a\ncontradiction between two numbers. In the supercritical two-dimensional regime,\nthe normalized Keller–Segel second moment decreases at a constant rate. A\nnonnegative density cannot have a negative second moment, so a classical solution\nsatisfying the virial hypotheses cannot survive past the moment when that affine\nquantity hits zero.\n\nFor mass , defineFor the whole-space system with sensitivity one and the normalized Newtonian\npotential, the classical second-moment identity is \n(Blanchet–Dolbeault–Perthame, Electron. J. Differential Equations\n2006(44), Theorem 1.1 and equation (2.3)).The pointwise Keller–Segel solution predicate does not fix this\nnormalization: adding a harmonic function to  preserves\n but can change the drift. A PDE derivation of the sharp\nvirial law therefore also needs the Newtonian normalization or equivalent\ndecay, finite moments, mass conservation, differentiation under the integral,\nand justified integration by parts. The theorems take the resulting scalar\nderivative identity as a hypothesis.\n\nA constant derivative integrates to an affine function, and that is all the\naffine law extracts from the identity; no sign condition on the mass is\nneeded for it.\n\nIf  is continuous on , differentiable on , and satisfies\nthe virial identity there, then\n\nRewrite the assumed derivative as the constant . The lower and\nupper mean-value inequalities on the convex interval  then bound\n from both sides by . The two bounds coincide,\nwhich gives the affine identity.\n\nSupercritical mass enters at this point, through : the affine\nfunction is then strictly decreasing and crosses zero at\n.\n\nSuppose , the second moment is continuous on ,\ndifferentiable on , and satisfies the virial identity there. If\n and the second moment is nonnegative at , thenThe endpoint  is any time up to which the hypotheses hold. Jäger–Luckhaus,\nTrans. Amer. Math. Soc. 329 (1992), DOI 10.1090/S0002-9947-1992-1046835-6,\nis the historical background for radial explosion arguments; the whole-space\nidentity is the one of Blanchet–Dolbeault–Perthame.\n\nSupercritical mass makes  positive. If the endpoint lay strictly\nbeyond , the affine formula at  would give\n. This contradicts the assumed nonnegativity of the\nendpoint moment.\n\nThe scalar theorem never mentions a density. The density-level version\nderives the one hypothesis the scalar statement cannot see, nonnegativity of\n, from pointwise nonnegativity of the slice at .\n\nFor a density , define its mass and\nsecond moment directly byThese are totalized Bochner integrals. The theorem assumes  and a\nfinite quadratic moment on every slice in ; the nonzero conserved\nmass certifies integrability of the density slices. If  is\npointwise nonnegative, , and  has the required\ncontinuity, differentiability, and virial identity, thenMass conservation and the differentiated virial law are hypotheses.\n\nMass conservation rewrites the differentiated density-level virial identity\nas the scalar identity with the fixed mass . Integrability of the\nquadratic moment and pointwise nonnegativity at  imply\n by monotonicity of the integral. The scalar endpoint theorem\nthen yields the displayed bound.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity\u0009Keller–Segel Virial Horizon","header":"6.7.1.8.1. Two-dimensional Keller–Segel virial horizon","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Keller___Segel-Virial-Horizon--Two-dimensional-Keller___Segel-virial-horizon"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.6. Exact Symbolic Calculus","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Exact-Symbolic-Calculus"}});